Partition Granularity
¶
Hive storage partitions data by time. Choose granularity based on your data
 Date format examples: 
 Date format Example YYYY-MM 2024-02 YYYY-MM-DD 2024-02-13 YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm 2024-02-13 00:00 YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm:ss 2024-02-13 00:00:00 YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm:ss.SSS 2024-02-13 00:00:00.000 YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm:ss.SSSZ 2024-02-13 00:00:00.000+00:00 
 You can construct your own format by combining the available tokens. Full list of all available tokens and formats can be found here (opens in a new tab) . 

 Example (ISO date format) 
 Returns the dates in ISO 8601 (opens in a new tab) 
 npx dukascopy-node -i eurusd -from 2024 - 0 2 - 13 -to 2024 - 0 2 - 15 -t m1 -f csv --date-format iso 
 output: 
 timestamp,open,high,low,close 
 2024-02-13T00:00:00.000Z,1.07718,1.07725,1.07718,1.07723 
 2024-02-13T00:01:00.000Z,1.07724,1.07726,1.07723,1.07726 
 2024-02-13T00:02:00.000Z,1.07727,1.07727,1.07722,1.07722 
 2024-02-13T00:03:00.000Z,1.07722,1.07727,1.07722,1.07727 
 2024-02-13T00:04:00.000Z,1.07728,1.07728,1.07727,1.07727 
 ... 
 Timezone conversion 
 By default, the dates are returned in UTC timezone. You can convert the dates to a different timezone by setting the --time-zone option. This option accepts a string that is compatible with the dayjs (opens in a new tab) library. This parameter only works in combination with the --date-format option. 
 Example 
 npx dukascopy-node -i eurusd -from 2024 - 0 2 - 13 -to 2024 - 0 2 - 15 -t m1 -f csv --date-format &quot;YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm&quot; --time-zone Europe/Kiev 
 output: 
 timestamp,open,high,low,close 
 2024-02-13 02:00,1.07718,1.07725,1.07718,1.07723 
 2024-02-13 02:01,1.07724,1.07726,1.07723,1.07726 
